steps.message:
The build isn't passing `-D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64` on the command line; it relies on `lfs.h` being included before the system headers. So to keep off_t consistent (64-bit) in treefiles.c, it should include "lfs.h" first — nbdsrv.c does this via nbdsrv.h. Wait no: nbdsrv.h includes lfs.h first. In treefiles.c, include "lfs.h" before the system headers. This is the safe, project-conforming approach (this is exactly why lfs.h exists).

Hmm, but wait — is that consistent with upstream? Upstream's treefiles.c doesn't include lfs.h. Let me check upstream more carefully… Actually, in the upstream repository, treefiles.c includes "config.h", then cliserv.h. cliserv.h itself has:

```c
#if NBD_LFS==1
#ifndef _LARGEFILE_SOURCE
#define _LARGEFILE_SOURCE
#endif
#define _FILE_OFFSET_BITS 64
#endif
```

However, this is after including <errno.h>, <string.h>, <netdb.h>, etc. Hmm, in cliserv.h these system includes come before the _FILE_OFFSET_BITS definition… but wait, cliserv.h includes "nbd.h" after those… Actually, the ordering issue: _FILE_OFFSET_BITS only has an effect if it's defined before any libc header is included. cliserv.h first includes <errno.h> and so on. Hmm, but on glibc, redefining _FILE_OFFSET_BITS partway through compilation still affects subsequent header inclusions, and off_t is defined in <sys/types.h>… tricky. In treefiles.c, include lfs.h first (the comment at the top of nbd-server.c says "include LFS defines, which defines behaviours of some of the following headers, so must come before those"). This is the project's own convention, and it's the safest option.

Also, treefiles.h includes <pthread.h> and <sys/types.h> — if lfs.h comes first, those are fine.

Now let me write treefiles.c. Contents (adapted from upstream, with the lfs.h include added):

```c
/*
 * Tree files support
 *
 * Store the data of an export in a tree of small block files instead of
 * a single large file. This is useful to provide a virtual block device
 * on an underlying filesystem that does not handle large files well,
 * for example fuse/ftpfs, davfs or other network filesystems.
 */

/* Includes LFS defines, which defines behaviours of some of the following
 * headers, so must come before those */
#include "lfs.h"

#include <errno.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <inttypes.h>
